   "text": {
    "L1": "A bar graph makes comparisons quick. The height of each bar shows its value on the axis.",
    "L2": "Let us use runs scored by four batters: 25, 40, 15 and 35.",
    "L3": "The biggest value is 40, so choose a scale of 1 unit equals 5 runs. Then 40 needs 8 units.",
    "L4": "Start the value axis at zero and mark 0, 5, 10, and so on up to 40.",
    "L5": "The bar heights are 5, 8, 3 and 7 units. Read each height against the scale, not by counting squares alone.",
    "L6": "Here is a myth: wider bars are fine if the value is bigger. Not true—bars must have the same width and equal gaps.",
    "L7": "A fair graph uses a clear title and labels, a suitable scale, and heights that match the values. Now choose the scale that fits."
   },
